import Foundation

/// A Mastodon's search (#319), **as a real server sent it**: the Mastodon 4.6.6 image
/// `servers/compose.yml` pins, the web process alone and with no full-text index, as
/// `servers/mastodon/seed.rb` left it. Fetched with `curl` on 2026-10-11 with no sign-in, with
/// this app's reading sign-in, with a sign-in that may do everything, with one made for reading
/// posts alone, and with a token that is none. The server was thrown away afterwards.
///
/// **Whole, not trimmed.** Each body is every byte the server sent. No token and nobody real
/// is in here: the accounts are the seed's.
///
/// ## What was asked, and what the server answered
///
/// | Asked | Answered |
/// | ----- | -------- |
/// | `/api/v2/search?q=…` with no kind named | 200, one body of `accounts`, `statuses`, `hashtags` (and a `collections` this app does not read), each matched by itself: everything comes back together. `type=accounts`, `type=hashtags`, `type=statuses` give that kind alone and the others empty; a kind it does not know (`type=bogus`) gives all of them empty |
/// | how long each kind is | twenty unasked. `limit` holds every kind at once: `limit=2` gave two accounts and the one hashtag there was. `limit=40`, `41` and `80` each gave all twenty-two people there were, so no cap could be seen here — forty is what this app asks for, as the server's documents say. `limit=0`, and a limit that is no number: every kind empty, **which is not "found nothing"** |
/// | how it goes on | **no `Link` header, and nothing in the body**: only `offset`, and **only with a kind named** — `type=accounts&limit=2`, then `&offset=2`, then `&offset=4` are three stretches of two, nobody twice. With no kind named `offset` is ignored and the first stretch comes again. `max_id` and `min_id` are ignored. An offset past the end: every kind empty. An offset of less than none: **400** `Pagination values for offset and limit must be positive` |
/// | no words (`q=`, spaces alone, or no `q`) | **400** `param is missing or the value is empty or invalid: q` |
/// | five thousand letters | 200, every kind empty |
/// | hashtags | found by the beginning of the name, whatever case, with or without the mark: `fediqo`, `fediqorising` and `#FediqoRising` each find `FediqoRising`. Each is the shape a rising hashtag has — `name` without the mark, `history` a day at a time with every figure a string — with `following` and `featuring` beside it |
/// | asked with no sign-in | people and hashtags are given to anyone (in another order: signed in, people the reader has to do with come first). **`resolve=true`: 401** `Search queries that resolve remote resources are not supported without authentication`. **`offset`: 401** `Search queries pagination is not supported without authentication` |
/// | a token that is none | answered as nobody is: 200 without `resolve`, that same 401 with it |
/// | a sign-in without `read:search` (made for `read:statuses`) | **403** `This action is outside the authorized scopes`, whatever is asked; the `WWW-Authenticate` header names what it wants: `read read:search` |
/// | somebody suspended; somebody who moved | **not found at all**, signed in or not |
/// | somebody the server limited | found, by anyone, with `"limited":true` |
/// | somebody the reader blocks or mutes, and somebody who blocks the reader | **found, signed in, exactly as anyone is given them**: nothing in the answer marks them, and nobody is left out — unlike a list of who follows whom, or the directory |
/// | an address of somebody who lives there (`@name@thishost`) | found with and without `resolve`, signed in or not. The address of their page (`https://thishost/@name`, `/users/name`): **nobody without `resolve`**, found with it |
/// | an address of somebody of another server it has already heard of (`@far@elsewhere.example`) | found without `resolve` — and, here, **nobody with it**: asked to resolve, the server went to ask their own server, which this one cannot reach, and answered `200` with every kind empty, saying nothing of why. The address of their page likewise; the address the server files them under itself (`https://elsewhere.example/users/far`) is found with `resolve`, with nothing fetched |
/// | an address it has never heard of (`@nobody@nowhere.example`, `https://nowhere.example/@nobody`) | 200, every kind empty, with and without `resolve`. **A server that cannot be reached, a server that says there is no such person, and a server never asked all arrive alike.** What a look-up brings from a server that can be reached could not be asked here: this one reaches no other |
/// | the address of a post, with `resolve=true` | with `type=accounts`: every kind empty — but with no kind named, or `type=statuses`, the post. So the kind named is what keeps a look-up of a person from bringing a post, and only a person's address is ever sent |
/// | how often | `x-ratelimit-limit: 300` in five minutes, counted down by every ask of it, signed in or not; `cache-control: private, no-store` |
///
/// So one ask with no kind named brings posts, people and hashtags together; the people found
/// are read on by a count of people passed over, with their kind named; and asking a source to
/// look an address up is a signed-in ask whose "nobody" says nothing of whether anybody was
/// reached.
